or personal data.\nL31: \nL32: [Input]\nL33:   * cite0†☰ L34:   *     *     * cite1†Home L35:     * Browse\nL36:       * cite2†Titles L37:       * cite3†Agencies L38:     * CFR Updates\nL39:       * cite4†Recent Changes L40:       * cite5†Issue Summaries L41:       * cite6†Corrections L42:     * Finding Aids\nL43:       * cite7†Parallel Table of Authorities L44:       * cite8†Subject Index L45:       * cite9†Agency-prepared Indexes L46:     * Reader Aids\nL47:       * cite10†Reader Aids Home L48:       * cite11†Using the eCFR Point-in-Time System L49:       * cite12†Understanding the eCFR L50:       * cite13†Government Policy and OFR Procedures L51:       * cite14†Developer Resources L52:       * cite15†Recent Site Updates L53:     * My eCFR\nL54:       * cite16†Sign In / Sign Up L55:     * [Input] [Input: Search the eCFR]\nL56: # cite1†eCFR L57: \nL58: ## The Electronic Code of Federal Regulations\nL59: \nL60: ###### Enhanced Content :: FR Reference\nL61: \nL62: Enhanced content is provided to the user to provide additional context.\nL63: \nL64: ###### Enhanced Content :: FR Reference\nL65: # Title 47\nL66: \nL67: This content is from the eCFR and is authoritative but unofficial.\nL68: \nL69: Displaying title 47, up to date as of 9/29/2026. Title 47 was last amended 9/29/2026.\nL70: \nL71: cite0†view historical versions L72: \nL73: A cite17†drafting site is available†drafting.ecfr.gov for use when drafting amendatory language\nL74: \nL75: cite17†switch to drafting site†drafting.ecfr.gov L76: \nL77: New Finding Aids are now available on eCFR.gov. The Parallel Table of Authorities, Subject Index, and Agency-prepared Indexes are now online! cite18†Learn more L78: Navigate by entering citations or phrases (eg: 1 CFR 1.1 49 CFR 172.101 Organization and Purpose 1/1.1 Regulation Y FAR).\nL79: \nL80: Choosing an item from citations and headings will bring you directly to the content. Choosing an item from full text search results will bring you to those results. Pressing enter in the search box will also bring you to search results.\nL81: \nL82: Background and more details are available in the cite19†Search & Navigation guide.\nL83: \nL84: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL85: [Input: Enter a search term or CFR reference (eg. fishing or 1 CFR 1.1)]\nL86: \nL87: [Input: Enter a search term or CFR reference (eg. fishing or 1 CFR 1.1)]\nL88: \nL89:   *   * Table of Contents\nL90:   * Details\nL91:   * Print/PDF\nL92:   * Display Options\nL93:   *   * Timeline\nL94:   * Go to Date\nL95:   * Compare Dates\nL96:   *   * Published Edition\nL97:   *   * Developer Tools\nL98: ###### eCFR Content\nL99: \nL100: The Code of Federal Regulations (CFR) is the official legal print publication containing the codification of the general and permanent rules published in the Federal Register by the departments and agencies of the Federal Government. The Electronic Code of Federal Regulations (eCFR) is a continuously updated online version of the CFR. It is not an official legal edition of the CFR.\nL101: \nL102: cite20†Learn more about the eCFR, its status, and the editorial process.\nL103: ###### Enhanced Content\nL104: \nL105: View table of contents for this page.\nL106: \nL107: ## Subpart N—Cable Rate Regulation\nL108: \nL109: #### Source:\nL110: \nL111: cite21†58 FR 29753†www.federalregister.gov , May 21, 1993, unless otherwise noted.\nL112: \nL113: ###### Effective Date Note\nL114: #### Effective Date Note:\nL115: The effective date of the amendments to part 76, published at cite22†58 FR 29737†www.federalregister.gov (May 21, 1993), extended to October 1, 1993, by an order published at cite23†58 FR 33560†www.federalregister.gov (June 18, 1993), and moved to September 1, 1993, by an order published at cite24†58 FR 41042†www.federalregister.gov (August 2, 1993), is temporarily stayed for those cable systems that have 1,000 or fewer subscribers.\nL116: This limited, temporary stay is effective September 1, 1993, and will remain in effect until the Commission terminates the stay and establishes a new effective date in an order on reconsideration addressing the administrative burdens and costs of compliance for small cable systems. The Commission will publish in the Federal Register the new effective date of the rules with respect to small cable systems at that time.\nL117: #### § 76.901 Definitions.\nL118: (a) Basic service.\nL119: The basic service tier shall, at a minimum, include all signals of domestic television broadcast stations provided to any subscriber (except a signal secondarily transmitted by satellite carrier beyond the local service area of such station, regardless of how such signal is ultimately received by the cable system) any public, educational, and governmental programming required by the franchise to be carried on the basic tier, and any additional video programming signals a service added to the basic tier by the cable operator.\nL120: (b) Cable programming service. Cable programming service includes any video programming provided over a cable system, regardless of service tier, including installation or rental of equipment used for the receipt of such video programming, other than:\nL121: \nL122: (1) Video programming carried on the basic service tier as defined in this section;\nL123: \nL124: (2) Video programming offered on a pay-per-channel or pay-per-program basis; or\nL125: (3) A combination of multiple channels of pay-per-channel or pay-per-program video programming offered on a multiplexed or time-shifted basis so long as the combined service:\nL126: \nL127: (i) Consists of commonly-identified video programming; and\nL128: \nL129: (ii) Is not bundled with any regulated tier of service.\nL130: (c) Small system. A small system is a cable television system that serves 15,000 or fewer subscribers. The service area of a small system shall be determined by the number of subscribers that are served by the system's principal headend, including any other headends or microwave receive sites that are technically integrated to the principal headend.\nL131: (d) Small cable company. A small cable company is a cable television operator that serves a total of 400,000 or fewer subscribers over one or more cable systems.\nL132: (e) Small cable operator. A small cable operator is an operator that, directly or through an affiliate, serves in the aggregate fewer than 1 percent of all subscribers in the United States and is not affiliated with any entity or entities whose gross annual revenues in the aggregate exceed $250,000,000.\nL133: For purposes of this definition, an operator shall be deemed affiliated with another entity if that entity holds a 20 percent or greater equity interest (not including truly passive investment) in the operator or exercises de jure or de facto control over the operator.\nL134: (1) Using the most reliable sources publicly available, the Commission periodically will determine and give public notice of the subscriber count that will serve as the 1 percent threshold until a new number is calculated.\nL135: \nL136: (2) For a discussion of passive interests with respect to small cable operators, see Implementation of Cable Act Reform Provisions of the Telecommunications Act of 1996, Report and Order in CS Docket No. 96-85, FCC 99-57 (released March 29, 1999).\nL137: (3) If two or more entities unaffiliated with each other each hold an equity interest in the small cable operator, the equity interests of the unaffiliated entities will not be aggregated with each other for the purpose of determining whether an entity meets or passes the 20 percent affiliation threshold.\nL138: [cite21†58 FR 29753†www.federalregister.gov , May 21, 1993, as amended at cite25†59 FR 62623†www.federalregister.gov , Dec. 6, 1994; cite26†60 FR 35864†www.federalregister.gov , July 12, 1995; cite27†64 FR 35950†www.federalregister.gov , July 2, 1999; cite28†83 FR 60775†www.federalregister.gov , Nov. 27, 2018]\nL139: #### § 76.905 Standards for identification of cable systems subject to effective competition.\nL140: \nL141: (a) Only the rates of cable systems that are not subject to effective competition may be regulated.\nL142: \nL143: (b) A cable system is subject to effective competition when any one of the following conditions is met:\nL144: \nL145: (1) Fewer than 30 percent of the households in its franchise area subscribe to the cable service of a cable system.\nL146: \nL147: (2) The franchise area is:\nL148: (i) Served by at least two unaffiliated multichannel video programming distributors each of which offers comparable programming to at least 50 percent of the households in the franchise area; and\nL149: \nL150: (ii) the number of households subscribing to multichannel video programming other than the largest multichannel video programming distributor exceeds 15 percent of the households in the franchise area.\nL151: (3) A multichannel video programming distributor, operated by the franchising authority for that franchise area, offers video programming to at least 50 percent of the households in the franchise area.\nL152: (4) A local exchange carrier or its affiliate (or any multichannel video programming distributor using the facilities of such carrier or its affiliate) offers video programming services directly to subscribers by any means (other than direct-to-home satellite services) in the franchise area of an unaffiliated cable operator which is providing cable service in that franchise area, but only if the video programming services so offered in that area are comparable to the video programming services provided by the unaffiliated cable operator in that area.\nL153: (c) For purposes of cite29†paragraphs (b)(1) through cite30†(b)(3) of this section, each separately billed or billable customer will count as a household subscribing to or being offered video programming services, with the exception of multiple dwelling buildings billed as a single customer. Individual units of multiple dwelling buildings will count as separate households. The term “households” shall not include those dwellings that are used solely for seasonal, occasional, or recreational use.\nL154: (d) A multichannel video program distributor, for purposes of this section, is an entity such as, but not limited to, a cable operator, a BRS/EBS provider, a direct broadcast satellite service, a television receive-only satellite program distributor, a video dialtone service provider, or a satellite master antenna television service provider that makes available for purchase, by subscribers or customers, multiple channels of video programming.\nL155: (e) Service of a multichannel video programming distributor will be deemed offered:\nL156: \nL157: (1) When the multichannel video programming distributor is physically able to deliver service to potential subscribers, with the addition of no or only minimal additional investment by the distributor, in order for an individual subscriber to receive service; and\nL158: (2) When no regulatory, technical or other impediments to households taking service exist, and potential subscribers in the franchise area are reasonably aware that they may purchase the services of the multichannel video programming distributor.\nL159: (f) For purposes of determining the number of households subscribing to the services of a multichannel video programming distributor other than the largest multichannel video programming distributor, under cite31†paragraph (b)(2)(ii) of this section, the number of subscribers of all multichannel video programming distributors that offer service in the franchise area will be aggregated.\nL160: (g) In order to offer comparable programming as that term is used in this section, a competing multichannel video programming distributor must offer at least 12 channels of video programming, including at least one channel of nonbroadcast service programming.\nL161: (h) For purposes of cite32†paragraph (b)(2) of this section, entities are affiliated if either entity has an attributable interest in the other or if a third party has an attributable interest in both entities. Attributable interest shall be defined by reference to the criteria set forth in Notes 1 through 5 to cite33†§ 76.501 .\nL162: (i) For purposes of cite34†paragraph (b)(4) of this section, entities are affiliated if either entity has an attributable interest in the other or if a third party has an attributable interest in both entities. Attributable interest shall be defined as follows:\nL163: \nL164: (1) A 10% partnership or voting equity interest in a corporation will be cognizable.\nL165: (2) Subject to paragraph (i)(3), a limited partnership interest of 10% or more shall be attributed to a limited partner unless that partner is not materially involved, directly or indirectly, in the management or operation of the media-related activities of the partnership and the relevant entity so certifies.\nL166: An interest in a Limited Liability Company (“LLC”) or Registered Limited Liability Partnership (“RLLP”) shall be attributed to the interest holder unless that interest holder is not materially involved, directly or indirectly, in the management or operation of the media-related activities of the partnership and the relevant entity so certifies. Certifications must be made pursuant to the guidelines set forth in Note 2(f) to cite33†§ 76.501 .\nL167: (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (i)(2), the holder of an equity or debt interest or interests in an entity covered by this rule shall have that interest attributed if the equity (including all stockholdings, whether voting or nonvoting, common or preferred, and partnership interests) and debt interest or interests, in the aggregate, exceed 33 percent of the total asset value (all equity plus all debt) of that entity.\nL168: (4) Discrete ownership interests held by the same individual or entity will be aggregated in determining whether or not an interest is cognizable under this section. An individual or entity will be deemed to have a cognizable investment if the sum of the interests other than those held by or through “passive investors” is equal to or exceeds 10%.\nL169: [cite21†58 FR 29753†www.federalregister.gov , May 21, 1993, as amended at cite35†59 FR 17972†www.federalregister.gov , Apr. 15, 1994; cite36†61 FR 18978†www.federalregister.gov , Apr. 30, 1996; cite37†62 FR 6495†www.federalregister.gov , Feb. 12, 1997; cite27†64 FR 35950†www.federalregister.gov , July 2, 1999; cite38†64 FR 67196†www.federalregister.gov , Dec. 1, 1999; cite39†69 FR 72046†www.federalregister.gov , Dec. 10, 2004]\nL170: #### § 76.906 Presumption of effective competition.\nL171: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\neCFR :: 47 CFR Part 76 Subpart P -- Competitive Availability of Navigation Devices (https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-76/subpart-P)\nciteturn2409view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-76/subpart-P\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 209\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\neCFR :: 47 CFR Part 76 Subpart T -- Notices (https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-76/subpart-T)\nciteturn2409view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-76/subpart-T\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 310\n"
